(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cutaneous-viral-and-fungal-infections-quiz"]={"title":"Cutaneous Viral and Fungal Infections Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Where do plantar warts occur, and what do they form when they cluster?","choices":["On weight-bearing surfaces, and clustered together they form a single deep abscess","On the fingers and palms, and clustered together they form a periungual band","On weight-bearing surfaces, and clustered together they form a mosaic wart","On the dorsal foot, and clustered together they form a giant condyloma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — mosaic warts are clustered plantar warts. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 145"},{"q":"What causes warts, and which cells does the virus infect?","choices":["Human papillomavirus, which infects keratinocytes and remains confined to the epidermis","Human papillomavirus, which infects dermal fibroblasts and extends deep into the dermis","A poxvirus, which infects keratinocytes and remains confined to the epidermis","A herpesvirus, which infects sensory ganglia and reaches the skin along a nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a benign proliferation confined to the epidermis. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 138"},{"q":"How is varicella managed, and what must be avoided in children?","choices":["Supportive care; avoid aspirin in children, and use caution with non-steroidal anti-inflammatories","Oral acyclovir for every patient regardless of age; avoid paracetamol in children, and limit oral antihistamines","Topical antivirals applied to each crop of lesions; avoid all analgesia in children, and use cool compresses alone","Systemic corticosteroids until the lesions crust; avoid antihistamines in children, and use caution with emollients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — consider early oral antivirals for higher-risk patients, and intravenous acyclovir for severe or disseminated disease. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"What is the first move in treating candidal intertrigo?","choices":["Apply a potent topical corticosteroid to settle the inflammation","Take a bacterial culture and start antibiotics empirically","Correct the environment first","Start an oral antifungal before any topical measure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — gently dry the folds, reduce friction and occlusion, use moisture-wicking fabric or absorbent material, and address incontinence or hyperhidrosis. The environment is what created the problem. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What must be protected in Ramsay Hunt syndrome, and why?","choices":["The facial nerve, by avoiding corticosteroids","The cornea, if eyelid closure is impaired","The tympanic membrane, if vesicles involve the canal","The inner ear, by avoiding all antivirals"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the facial palsy can prevent the eye from closing. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"How does vitiligo differ from pityriasis versicolor on the deck's differential?","choices":["Vitiligo shows short hyphae with yeast clusters on microscopy","Vitiligo is completely white — depigmented — and is an autoimmune disorder","Vitiligo has an erythematous yellowish tint with soft greasy scales","Vitiligo begins with a herald patch and spreads in a Christmas tree pattern"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — pityriasis versicolor is hypopigmented rather than depigmented, and it scales. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which single feature is characteristic of the molluscum lesion?","choices":["A thrombosed capillary dot","A shallow painful ulcer","Central umbilication","An advancing scaly border"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the deck calls it characteristic. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 134"},{"q":"Which oral antivirals are preferred for herpes zoster, and when should treatment start?","choices":["Valacyclovir, famciclovir or acyclovir; as soon as possible, ideally within 72 hours of rash onset","Valacyclovir only, since famciclovir and acyclovir are ineffective; within two weeks of rash onset at the latest","Topical acyclovir rather than any oral agent; as soon as the prodrome begins, and never once the rash has appeared","Oral corticosteroid alone, with antivirals reserved for relapse; within 72 hours of the rash appearing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — adjust for renal function and interactions. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"What is black dot tinea capitis?","choices":["Infection with punctate haemorrhage into the scalp at follicular sites","Infection with fracture of the hair at the scalp surface","Infection producing black comedone-like plugs within the follicular openings","Infection with black pigment produced by the dermatophyte itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the black dots are the broken hair shafts, and patches of alopecia carrying them are characteristic. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which two treatments does the deck give for plantar warts?","choices":["Berdazimer 10.3% gel and topical retinoid","Oral acyclovir and topical lidocaine","Salicylic acid 40% and cryotherapy","Cantharidin 0.7% and curettage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the two named options. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 146"},{"q":"How far beyond the lesion border should topical antifungal be applied in tinea corporis?","choices":["Five centimetres beyond the border","To the whole affected limb","One to two centimetres beyond the border","To the lesion only, avoiding normal skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — with topical terbinafine, butenafine or an azole for localized disease. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How does vesiculobullous tinea pedis present?","choices":["A dry, chronic form that is asymptomatic, with diffuse thickening of the sole","A macerated form confined to the web spaces with fissuring","A pustular form with follicular involvement of the dorsum","A moist, acute, pruritic and painful vesicular or bullous eruption"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — on underlying erythema, and caused by Trichophyton species like the other two forms. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which wart cases does the deck say to refer?","choices":["Periungual, facial, extensive, recalcitrant, diagnostically uncertain, or immunocompromised cases","Only plantar warts that have been present for more than one year and have failed salicylic acid","Only warts in children under the age of five years","Only mosaic warts that have failed salicylic acid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — six referral categories. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 149"},{"q":"What does a potassium hydroxide preparation show in pityriasis versicolor?","choices":["Branching septate hyphae only, with no yeast forms","Budding yeast with pseudohyphae and no true hyphae","No fungal elements at all, since the diagnosis is purely clinical","Short hyphae with clusters of yeast — spaghetti and meatballs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a Wood lamp may show yellow-gold fluorescence but sensitivity is limited. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What causes herpes zoster, and what raises the risk?","choices":["An autoimmune response to a previous varicella infection; risk rises with atopy and connective-tissue disease","Reactivation of latent varicella-zoster virus; risk rises with age and impaired cell-mediated immunity","A new infection with varicella-zoster virus caught from an infected contact; risk rises with crowding and poor hygiene","Reactivation of latent herpes simplex virus in a sensory ganglion; risk rises with ultraviolet exposure and febrile illness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the virus stays latent in cranial-nerve or dorsal-root ganglia. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"When should polymerase chain reaction be used in suspected herpes zoster, and from what?","choices":["Only in patients aged over 50 with typical disease; from paired serology rather than from the lesion itself","For atypical, disseminated, vaccine-modified or immunocompromised presentations; from vesicle fluid, scab, or cells from the lesion base","In every case before starting antivirals, whatever the presentation; from a blood sample rather than from the lesion or its vesicle fluid","Only after antiviral therapy has failed, and never at presentation; from a full-thickness skin biopsy rather than from vesicle fluid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — typical unilateral dermatomal vesicles are diagnosed clinically. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"Which examination finding separates tinea barbae from bacterial folliculitis?","choices":["The hair is easily removed in tinea barbae","The lesions are painful in tinea barbae and painless in folliculitis","Tinea barbae spares the upper lip","Tinea barbae produces fever and lymphadenopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and bacterial culture can be performed to rule folliculitis out. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Who should receive recombinant zoster vaccine, and how many doses?","choices":["Two doses for immunocompetent adults aged 50 and over, and two doses for adults aged 19 and over who are or will be immunodeficient or immunosuppressed","One dose for immunocompetent adults aged 65 and over, and one dose for adults aged 19 and over who are or will be immunodeficient or immunosuppressed","Two doses only for adults who have never had chickenpox, and no doses at all for anyone with a history of varicella or of prior zoster","Two doses for immunocompetent adults aged 19 and over, and none for adults who are or will be immunodeficient or immunosuppressed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — standard interval two to six months. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"What is first-line therapy for most dermatophyte onychomycosis, and for how long?","choices":["Oral fluconazole for six months, which is the licensed option in the United States","Oral terbinafine, usually six weeks for fingernails and twelve weeks for toenails","Oral terbinafine, usually twelve weeks for fingernails and six weeks for toenails","Topical ciclopirox for six weeks regardless of the nail involved"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — review hepatic disease and interactions, and obtain baseline liver tests per labelling and patient risk. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What should be done with a low-positive herpes simplex type 2 serology result?","choices":["Accept it as diagnostic and begin suppressive therapy","Discard it and repeat the identical assay in six months","Confirm it with an immunoglobulin M level on the same sample","Confirm it with a second method when one is available"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — low-positive results may be false positives. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"How is postherpetic neuralgia defined, and how might the pain be described?","choices":["Pain beginning more than 90 days after the rash has resolved; aching or stabbing, but never evoked by light touch","Any pain at all during the acute eruptive phase; burning, aching or electric shock-like in quality","Pain persisting 90 days or more after rash onset; burning, aching, stabbing, electric shock-like, or evoked by light touch","Pain persisting 14 days or more after the rash appears; purely burning in quality, never stabbing and never provoked by light touch"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — pain evoked by light touch is allodynia. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which piece of tinea pedis patient education does the deck mark as essential?","choices":["Apply a corticosteroid cream to reduce itching","Dry between the toes after bathing","Wear closed shoes to keep the feet clean","Soak the feet daily in warm water"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — alongside antifungal foot powder for shoes, open-toed sandals when possible, sandals in community showers, and changing socks frequently. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"How is an id reaction treated?","choices":["It resolves when the primary dermatophyte infection is treated","With a potent topical corticosteroid to the distant site","With oral antifungal directed at the distant site","With oral antihistamines alone until it settles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — treating the distant eruption itself is not the answer. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What does the deck warn against for benign warts likely to resolve on their own?","choices":["Salicylic acid at any concentration","Excessive freezing or destructive therapy","Any period of observation without treatment","Trimming the surface of the lesion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — do not over-treat a self-limiting lesion. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 149"},{"q":"What is first-line therapy for pityriasis versicolor, and what is one named regimen?","choices":["Oral fluconazole for seven days, in every case regardless of extent","Oral terbinafine taken for a course of two weeks","Topical nystatin applied twice daily for a fortnight","Topical therapy; selenium sulfide daily for seven days"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — regimens vary by product. Ketoconazole shampoo or cream, zinc pyrithione, ciclopirox and topical terbinafine are the other options. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"How does herpetic whitlow present?","choices":["Prodromal burning or tingling, then grouped vesicles on an erythematous swollen digit, sometimes with fever or lymphangitis","Painless swelling of the whole hand, then a single large bulla on the palm, sometimes with joint stiffness","Sudden nail plate separation, then thickening and yellow discolouration, sometimes with subungual debris","A slowly enlarging painless nodule on the finger, then central umbilication, sometimes with satellite lesions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that is the sequence the deck gives. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"How does the allylamine class of antifungals work, and how do you recognise one by name?","choices":["It blocks synthesis of ergosterol; the names end in -azole","It inhibits mitosis in dividing fungal cells; the names end in -fulvin","It blocks cell wall glucan synthesis; the names end in -fungin","It destroys the fungal cell membrane; the names end in -fine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — terbinafine and naftifine. Destroying the membrane prevents growth and ultimately kills the fungus. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What kind of virus is herpes simplex, and what defines its behaviour in the host?","choices":["A single-stranded RNA virus of the Poxviridae family that produces latent but lifelong infection","A double-stranded DNA virus of the Papillomaviridae family that is cleared once lesions resolve","A single-stranded DNA virus of the Herpesviridae family that is confined entirely to the epidermis","A double-stranded DNA virus of the Herpesviridae family that produces latent but lifelong infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — latency for life is the defining feature. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"A child has genital molluscum lesions. What does the deck say about interpreting this?","choices":["It confirms sexual abuse, so a report should be filed on the location alone","It excludes sexual abuse, because genital lesions in children are always autoinoculated","It requires no assessment at all, because genital lesions are expected in children","It requires context-sensitive assessment, because location alone does not prove abuse"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — location alone is not proof. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 133"},{"q":"How does the imidazole class work, and which two examples does the deck give?","choices":["It blocks synthesis of chitin in the fungal cell wall; nystatin and amphotericin","It inhibits fungal deoxyribonucleic acid synthesis; griseofulvin and fluconazole","It blocks ergosterol synthesis; clotrimazole and ketoconazole","It destroys the fungal cell membrane directly; terbinafine and naftifine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — ergosterol is a vital component of the fungal cell membrane, so blocking it halts growth and replication. The names end in -azole. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 5"}]};
